00:46:11.350 --> 00:46:17.330
And of course, you know, there had already been Lazy Sunday on SNL, so this was like inspired

1014
00:46:17.330 --> 00:46:22.410
by Lazy Sunday, and it made me laugh so hard when we watched it.

1015
00:46:23.250 --> 00:46:29.310
So many of Michael's bits are based on the bits of Saturday Night Live and or other comedians.

1016
00:46:29.450 --> 00:46:31.710
He really writes no new material.

1017
00:46:31.970 --> 00:46:33.830
He thinks he's a world-class comedian.

1018
00:46:34.050 --> 00:46:35.310
I mean, he—

1019
00:46:35.310 --> 00:46:39.890
Because he just restructures and redoes other people's bits.

1020
00:46:40.150 --> 00:46:43.490
But he truly thinks he's like an amazing comedian.

1021
00:46:43.490 --> 00:46:49.750
He even called this meeting, which should be just like a new employee orientation meeting,

1022
00:46:50.730 --> 00:46:52.930
he calls it his gigantic performance.

1023
00:46:53.770 --> 00:46:54.130
Yes.

1024
00:46:54.470 --> 00:46:54.970
Yes.

1025
00:46:55.250 --> 00:46:57.550
Michael reminds me of a cruise director.

1026
00:46:57.550 --> 00:46:58.550
Oh, gosh.

1027
00:46:58.910 --> 00:46:59.090
Wait.

1028
00:46:59.810 --> 00:47:00.610
That's perfect.

1029
00:47:00.830 --> 00:47:02.030
That's all you need to say.

1030
00:47:02.470 --> 00:47:05.910
I'm sure there's a story here, but that is the best example.

1031
00:47:06.930 --> 00:47:09.070
Michael is a cruise ship director.

1032
00:47:09.530 --> 00:47:09.930
Yeah.

1033
00:47:10.770 --> 00:47:11.190
What—

1034
00:47:11.190 --> 00:47:12.190
You have to tell me the story.

1035
00:47:12.190 --> 00:47:13.290
What happened on the cruise ship?

1036
00:47:13.890 --> 00:47:15.430
Well, I took a cruise.

1037
00:47:15.530 --> 00:47:17.730
This was when cruising was still happening.

1038
00:47:17.730 --> 00:47:31.450
And our cruise director, like, 15 years after Austin Powers had come out, his bit was to dress up like Austin Powers and be like,

1039
00:47:31.590 --> 00:47:32.510
Oh, behave.

1040
00:47:33.230 --> 00:47:34.210
Oh, my gosh.

1041
00:47:34.230 --> 00:47:38.390
And I remember thinking, like, has he been doing this bit for 15 years?

1042
00:47:38.650 --> 00:47:41.850
Or where is—we are—I don't know.

1043
00:47:41.970 --> 00:47:45.050
And then, you know, of course, there was some Borat thrown in there.

1044
00:47:45.050 --> 00:47:48.010
It was like—it was like watching Michael Scott live.

1045
00:47:48.210 --> 00:47:52.250
But I'll tell you what, by the end of the cruise, I—I don't know.

1046
00:47:52.350 --> 00:47:53.950
This guy, I was on board.

1047
00:47:54.170 --> 00:47:54.950
Like, by the end of the cruise—

1048
00:47:54.950 --> 00:47:55.250
He won you over?

1049
00:47:55.630 --> 00:47:57.630
He was like, yeah, baby, yeah.

1050
00:47:57.950 --> 00:47:59.250
You were like, I'm in.

1051
00:47:59.990 --> 00:48:06.170
By the end of the cruise, I was full-on doing, like, a scavenger hunt with my mom and my sister.

1052
00:48:06.210 --> 00:48:08.270
It was a cruise I took with my mom and sister.

1053
00:48:08.450 --> 00:48:10.010
It was like, you know, a—

1054
00:48:10.010 --> 00:48:11.070
Just a girl's trip.

1055
00:48:11.230 --> 00:48:12.250
Girl's trip, yeah.

1056
00:48:12.250 --> 00:48:17.870
Yeah, and we were with a bunch of other strangers, and we were doing this scavenger hunt.

1057
00:48:19.110 --> 00:48:22.530
And, like, my poor sister, she's going to be so embarrassed when I tell this story.

1058
00:48:24.110 --> 00:48:27.690
The thing we had to find was just, like, a piece of woman's clothing.

1059
00:48:27.950 --> 00:48:33.350
So my sister took off her bra and, like, took it out of her shirt and was, like, waving her hair.

1060
00:48:33.450 --> 00:48:34.650
And she's like, we got it!

1061
00:48:35.130 --> 00:48:36.890
And we were like, oh, behave!

1062
00:48:37.610 --> 00:48:38.670
Oh, my God.

1063
00:48:38.990 --> 00:48:40.010
It was insane.

1064
00:48:40.010 --> 00:48:41.530
I love that story, Jenna.

1065
00:48:41.770 --> 00:48:42.410
And you know what?

1066
00:48:43.050 --> 00:48:50.390
Having now competed with your family in Family Feud, I know how competitive everyone in your family is.

1067
00:48:50.510 --> 00:48:54.010
I believe that Emily was like, oh, we will win this if I have to take my bra off.

1068
00:48:54.070 --> 00:48:54.810
We're going to win!

1069
00:48:55.230 --> 00:48:56.130
She was.

1070
00:48:56.230 --> 00:48:56.910
She was.

1071
00:48:57.010 --> 00:49:00.290
And we didn't win, I'll say.

1072
00:49:00.470 --> 00:49:01.970
Well, sadly.

1073
00:49:02.190 --> 00:49:03.570
But we gave it our best effort.

1074
00:49:03.630 --> 00:49:04.170
You sure did.

1075
00:49:04.170 --> 00:49:09.670
All right, so we asked both Ken and Brent what they remembered about filming Lazy Scranton.

1076
00:49:09.790 --> 00:49:12.410
And here's what Ken Whittingham had to say.

1077
00:49:12.630 --> 00:49:20.810
One of my favorite scenes in the episode was creating the Lazy Scranton video, which was inspired from SNL's Lazy Sunday sketch.

1078
00:49:21.250 --> 00:49:28.830
We kind of went off script and created a bunch of really funny extra scenes to make the video really hard to watch for the Dunder Mifflin employees.

1079
00:49:28.830 --> 00:49:30.650
Unfortunately, we couldn't use all of them.

1080
00:49:30.930 --> 00:49:31.550
Hold up.

1081
00:49:31.650 --> 00:49:34.570
Are you telling me there's extra footage from this?

1082
00:49:34.930 --> 00:49:36.350
Yeah, there must be.

1083
00:49:36.530 --> 00:49:38.150
I need an extended cut.

1084
00:49:38.330 --> 00:49:41.370
I want the extended cut of Lazy Scranton.

1085
00:49:41.630 --> 00:49:42.530
I know.

1086
00:49:42.850 --> 00:49:49.610
I know because this was one of those moments where Rain and Steve got to go off on their own and shoot a bunch of stuff.

1087
00:49:49.610 --> 00:49:52.930
And we only got to see what the audience sees.

1088
00:49:53.070 --> 00:49:54.610
We only got to see that final version.

1089
00:49:54.970 --> 00:49:55.350
Yeah.

1090
00:49:56.150 --> 00:49:58.650
Oh, Ken, I want those extra scenes.

1091
00:49:59.030 --> 00:49:59.390
All right.

1092
00:49:59.450 --> 00:50:00.610
Well, we also asked Brent.

1093
00:50:00.850 --> 00:50:03.750
And here's what Brent remembers about shooting those scenes.

1094
00:50:03.990 --> 00:50:11.750
What I remember about shooting that Lazy Scranton video was just being in awe of Rainn Wilson and Steve Carell as performers.

1095
00:50:12.010 --> 00:50:14.030
These guys are such serious actors.

1096
00:50:14.350 --> 00:50:20.550
And between takes, they would be talking about this performance like they were the Royal Shakespeare Company.

1097
00:50:20.750 --> 00:50:22.430
And then the director would yell action.

1098
00:50:22.430 --> 00:50:27.090
And they would suddenly become these cartoon characters bouncing off the walls like Bugs Bunny.

1099
00:50:27.090 --> 00:50:31.110
And then cut and suddenly they're inside the actor's studio for about two minutes.

1100
00:50:31.290 --> 00:50:33.990
And then action and it's Benny Hill.

1101
00:50:34.190 --> 00:50:36.470
It was so wonderful to watch.

1102
00:50:36.690 --> 00:50:43.070
And it ended up, I think, in a kind of classic little bit of comedy film in the middle of the episode.

1103
00:50:43.070 --> 00:50:48.470
I wish we would have asked them also about the little clip of Michael doing the Scranton Witch Project.

1104
00:50:48.930 --> 00:50:49.410
Yes.

1105
00:50:50.130 --> 00:50:51.930
Because that's something else they filmed.

1106
00:50:52.090 --> 00:50:54.930
And I'm sure Steve did a whole bunch of stuff that didn't make it.

1107
00:50:55.510 --> 00:51:02.330
Well, speaking of stuff that didn't make it, Brent sent us in some insight into something that I saw in the script,

1108
00:51:02.630 --> 00:51:07.910
which was Michael went on to do a magic show for us after showing us Lazy Scranton.

1109
00:51:07.910 --> 00:51:09.750
But we'll let Brent tell you about it.

1110
00:51:10.270 --> 00:51:15.210
What I remember about filming this episode is that we ended up with too much comedy.

1111
00:51:15.450 --> 00:51:25.850
The premise for Michael Scott was so perfect that he would have these new employees and a new audience to perform for and impress that we just ended up writing too many comedy bits for him.

1112
00:51:25.890 --> 00:51:27.710
And I remember one that made us laugh so hard.

1113
00:51:27.710 --> 00:51:32.570
We had made a giant deck of cards for Michael Scott.

1114
00:51:32.830 --> 00:51:38.550
And in the conference room, he says, if you put your heart into your sales, you will be a king of hearts.

1115
00:51:38.770 --> 00:51:41.950
And then he has an employee pick a card, and it is the king of hearts.

1116
00:51:42.090 --> 00:51:47.850
And he says, and if you all put your hearts into your sales, then you will all be a king of hearts.

1117
00:51:47.990 --> 00:51:51.170
And he flips his deck of cards over, and they're all the king of hearts.

1118
00:51:51.170 --> 00:51:58.090
And then he acts like he's blown their minds when, in fact, he's just done the worst magic trick ever.

1119
00:51:58.330 --> 00:52:03.630
It was so funny, and we just had no room for it because the episode was so packed with good stuff.

1120
00:52:04.210 --> 00:52:05.770
Angela, I remember shooting that.

1121
00:52:06.050 --> 00:52:08.170
I remember the giant deck of cards.

1122
00:52:08.490 --> 00:52:10.030
Yeah, and how he fanned them all out.

1123
00:52:10.130 --> 00:52:11.930
Well, it's in the deleted scenes.

1124
00:52:12.410 --> 00:52:13.410
Oh, it is?

1125
00:52:13.550 --> 00:52:14.550
We can watch that.

1126
00:52:14.710 --> 00:52:15.290
Oh, good.

1127
00:52:16.110 --> 00:52:18.650
So if you have the DVDs, that one made it.

1128
00:52:18.650 --> 00:52:32.410
But I remember Steve's handling of the prop of the cards and the kings of hearts and how he did just, like, the worst magician hand work with the cards.

1129
00:52:32.630 --> 00:52:34.850
I just remember trying so hard not to laugh.

1130
00:52:35.090 --> 00:52:37.090
It was so hard to not laugh.

1131
00:52:37.290 --> 00:52:41.550
All right, lady, that kind of sums up the first conference room meeting.

1132
00:52:41.790 --> 00:52:46.310
Oh, there's going to be another one, but should we take a break and then we'll come back?

1133
00:52:46.310 --> 00:52:53.150
Yeah, let's take a break and come back because Andy continues to try to win over everybody.

1134
00:52:53.150 --> 00:52:54.810
We'll talk about that when we come back.

1135
00:52:55.550 --> 00:53:10.330
All right, so we are back, and at 15 minutes, Andy is hitting on Angela?

1136
00:53:10.690 --> 00:53:11.470
Is that what's happening?

1137
00:53:12.270 --> 00:53:12.890
Yeah.

1138
00:53:13.250 --> 00:53:18.110
He has put a cat dressed as a cowboy as his screensaver.

1139
00:53:18.310 --> 00:53:18.670
Yeah.

1140
00:53:18.810 --> 00:53:20.730
And he compliments your smile.

1141
00:53:21.310 --> 00:53:22.810
My what smile?

1142
00:53:23.310 --> 00:53:24.970
Look, look at my face.

1143
00:53:25.150 --> 00:53:31.170
I remember doing this scene, and I remember Ken Whittingham and Brent Forster and I just brainstorming a little bit.

1144
00:53:32.610 --> 00:53:35.930
How big would my actual smile be?

1145
00:53:36.210 --> 00:53:36.890
You know what I mean?

1146
00:53:36.970 --> 00:53:37.610
Like, sure.

1147
00:53:37.910 --> 00:53:39.770
It wouldn't be just like, it couldn't be a smirk.

1148
00:53:39.790 --> 00:53:41.090
It couldn't be a full smile.

1149
00:53:41.310 --> 00:53:47.490
It sort of was like this pursed lips, barely reacting, but slightly amused.

1150
00:53:48.670 --> 00:53:58.410
So we did a few takes of, like, me sort of, like, trying to not smile but seem like I'm somewhat happy, and that, like,

1151
00:53:58.410 --> 00:54:04.890
is something that I laugh thinking about because I remember Ken and Brent both being like, okay, maybe even less of a smile?

1152
00:54:05.050 --> 00:54:06.930
Or, okay, maybe you've got to do something.

1153
00:54:07.170 --> 00:54:10.090
Like, we were just, like, trying to dial it and find it.

1154
00:54:10.090 --> 00:54:11.850
But, well, I love where it landed.

1155
00:54:12.070 --> 00:54:16.950
I think it's perfect, and it is probably more smiling than we've ever seen from Angela Martin, so.

1156
00:54:17.670 --> 00:54:23.390
Well, I think the biggest smile my character has had is when she realizes that Dwight gave her a key.

1157
00:54:24.570 --> 00:54:25.030
Yes.

1158
00:54:25.630 --> 00:54:26.690
Yes, that's right.

1159
00:54:26.690 --> 00:54:29.850
But this is, like, smile, but this is, this is amused.

1160
00:54:30.210 --> 00:54:30.930
Yeah.

1161
00:54:31.190 --> 00:54:32.170
Slightly amused.

1162
00:54:33.310 --> 00:54:41.230
Besides your smile, I was very distracted at 15 minutes, 50 seconds with your all-brown outfit.

1163
00:54:41.690 --> 00:54:43.950
This is a new color for you.

1164
00:54:44.110 --> 00:54:46.490
You're usually in the gray tones.

1165
00:54:46.870 --> 00:54:47.230
Ugh.

1166
00:54:47.450 --> 00:54:55.550
These are striking moments because, like, Rain almost always wears mustard yellow, but I noticed in a recent episode he's in green.

1167
00:54:56.150 --> 00:54:58.030
He's, like, but it's monotone.

1168
00:54:58.270 --> 00:54:59.170
Like, that's the other thing.

1169
00:54:59.350 --> 00:55:02.310
Both you and Dwight dress in, like, a monotone.

1170
00:55:02.510 --> 00:55:02.830
Mm-hmm.

1171
00:55:03.010 --> 00:55:03.790
Have you noticed?

1172
00:55:04.250 --> 00:55:04.410
Yes.

1173
00:55:04.410 --> 00:55:06.210
It's, like, yeah.

1174
00:55:06.210 --> 00:55:07.510
Yes, absolutely.

1175
00:55:07.550 --> 00:55:15.690
I mean, it was a brown skirt, and then I had a brown button sweater over, like, a black shirt.

1176
00:55:15.910 --> 00:55:17.530
Like, it was so weird.

1177
00:55:17.730 --> 00:55:18.950
The whole outfit was so bizarre.

1178
00:55:19.330 --> 00:55:19.570
Yeah.

1179
00:55:20.050 --> 00:55:23.930
Should we move to the heartbreak in the break room?

1180
00:55:24.810 --> 00:55:26.270
Oh, lady.

1181
00:55:26.670 --> 00:55:29.470
Do you know what I titled this scene on my note card?

1182
00:55:29.850 --> 00:55:30.430
What?

1183
00:55:30.910 --> 00:55:31.950
Very hunchy.

1184
00:55:32.890 --> 00:55:34.170
Oh, yeah.

1185
00:55:34.730 --> 00:55:35.910
Very hunchy.

1186
00:55:36.490 --> 00:55:37.350
Posture, Jenna.

1187
00:55:37.930 --> 00:55:41.590
But it's so true, and you know why she's doing it.

1188
00:55:41.690 --> 00:55:42.790
Okay, Jenna, set up the scene.

1189
00:55:42.990 --> 00:55:50.530
Jim is getting a water instead of his usual grape soda from the vending machine, and Pam comes in,

1190
00:55:50.530 --> 00:55:54.410
and she is, finally, she has him alone, right?

1191
00:55:54.590 --> 00:55:59.610
So she wants their old banter back, and she's like, oh, no grape soda, huh?

1192
00:55:59.890 --> 00:56:00.150
Mm-hmm.

1193
00:56:00.170 --> 00:56:01.990
And he's like, yeah, no, just water.

1194
00:56:02.130 --> 00:56:04.190
He doesn't meet her there, right?

1195
00:56:04.390 --> 00:56:06.870
And she's like, oh, you've changed so much.

1196
00:56:06.870 --> 00:56:08.950
And he's like, oh, yeah, I don't know.

1197
00:56:09.190 --> 00:56:18.610
And then Michael comes in, and Pam tries to give Jim a little, like, look, a little bit like, oh, my gosh.

1198
00:56:19.050 --> 00:56:20.650
And Jim does not return.

1199
00:56:21.350 --> 00:56:21.570
No.

1200
00:56:21.870 --> 00:56:23.690
He is giving you nothing back.

1201
00:56:23.890 --> 00:56:27.790
And he says to Michael, you're not interrupting anything because nothing's happening.

1202
00:56:28.090 --> 00:56:31.030
It's kind of cold, and he just leaves.

1203
00:56:31.030 --> 00:56:33.050
And Pam was like, yeah.

1204
00:56:33.510 --> 00:56:41.350
And then, Jenna, it looked like if you could have crawled inside your sexy sweater that your mom had knitted you, you would have.

1205
00:56:41.350 --> 00:56:44.350
Because you hunch way into yourself.

1206
00:56:44.510 --> 00:56:46.950
And I was like, oh, Pam, so hunchy.

1207
00:56:47.130 --> 00:56:51.210
There's a country song here called Heartbreak in the Break Room Getting Hunchy.

1208
00:56:51.410 --> 00:56:55.630
I was wearing a sexy sweater that my mama gave me.

1209
00:56:55.630 --> 00:57:00.510
And then I went in the break room, and he broke me.

1210
00:57:01.070 --> 00:57:02.770
So what did I do?

1211
00:57:03.010 --> 00:57:06.030
I tried to crawl into my sexy sweater.

1212
00:57:06.990 --> 00:57:12.670
I got my heartbreak in the break room.

1213
00:57:13.650 --> 00:57:16.110
Our country music singing is really breathy.

1214
00:57:16.310 --> 00:57:18.930
It's not like peppy country.

1215
00:57:19.130 --> 00:57:23.470
Like, there's a heartbreak in the break room, and I'm going to tell you why.

1216
00:57:25.770 --> 00:57:26.950
Oh, good Lord.

1217
00:57:26.950 --> 00:57:27.370
Okay.

1218
00:57:27.730 --> 00:57:28.390
Oh, good Lord.

1219
00:57:28.890 --> 00:57:29.730
We should move on.

1220
00:57:29.730 --> 00:57:30.670
Hey, can I put it out there?

1221
00:57:30.930 --> 00:57:33.090
Anybody want to write heartbreak in the break room?

1222
00:57:34.010 --> 00:57:34.830
We'll play it.

1223
00:57:34.890 --> 00:57:35.830
Here are your keywords.

1224
00:57:36.190 --> 00:57:37.310
Heartbreak in the break room.

1225
00:57:38.830 --> 00:57:39.710
Very hunchy.

1226
00:57:40.450 --> 00:57:41.110
Sexy sweater.

1227
00:57:41.290 --> 00:57:42.210
Mama's sexy sweater.

1228
00:57:42.590 --> 00:57:46.730
Yeah, mama knit me a sexy sweater for my heartbreak in the break room.

1229
00:57:46.870 --> 00:57:47.810
Oh, there it is.

1230
00:57:48.150 --> 00:57:52.570
Mama knit me a sexy sweater, but all it got me was heartbreak in the break room.

1231
00:57:52.810 --> 00:57:54.730
Oh, come on.

1232
00:57:54.730 --> 00:57:58.790
All right, so now, where do we go from here?

1233
00:57:58.910 --> 00:58:00.230
I mean, where do we go from here?

1234
00:58:00.410 --> 00:58:02.770
Hannah is pumping in the bullpen.

1235
